::MCR_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
A grade uses quizzes at 40% and projects at 60%. Scores are 80 for quizzes and 90 for projects. What is the weighted average?
{
=A. 86#Correct. 80(0.40) + 90(0.60) \= 32 + 54 \= 86.
~B. 85#This is the simple average, but the categories have different weights.
~C. 170#This adds the scores without applying weights.
~D. 68#This applies weights incorrectly and does not add the weighted products.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Which decimal weight is equivalent to 30%?
{
~A. 3.0#This represents 300%, not 30%.
=B. 0.30#Correct. Divide 30 by 100 to write 30% as 0.30.
~C. 0.03#This represents 3%, not 30%.
~D. 30.0#This is far too large for a decimal weight.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
An option has scores 70, 85, and 95 with weights 50%, 30%, and 20%. What is the weighted score?
{
~A. 83.3#This is close to a simple average, but weights must be applied.
~B. 250#This adds the scores without applying weights.
=C. 79.5#Correct. 70(0.50) + 85(0.30) + 95(0.20) \= 35 + 25.5 + 19 \= 79.5.
~D. 95#This selects one score rather than calculating the weighted score.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Why should weights total 100%?
{
~A. So every score becomes the same#Weights do not make scores the same; they show category importance.
~B. So the largest score is always chosen#Weighted averages combine categories rather than choosing only the largest score.
~C. So no calculation is needed#The calculation is still needed after checking weights.
=D. So the full decision is accounted for#Correct. Weights totaling 100% represent the whole decision or whole grade.
}
::MCR_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U01_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.QR.3
Question:
Option A has a weighted score of 82. Option B has a weighted score of 79. Which option is better by weighted score?
{
~A. Option B, because 79 is smaller#A smaller weighted score is not better when higher scores represent stronger options.
=B. Option A, because 82 is greater than 79#Correct. By weighted score, the higher total indicates the stronger option.
~C. They are equal because both are weighted scores#The scores are not equal; 82 is greater than 79.
~D. There is no way to compare weighted scores#Weighted scores are designed to support comparisons.
}
